{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-344916",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-2)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)-f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2)}{(g(-2))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-2)=3$ and $g(-2)=2.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,-1)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,-1)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-2) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)-f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2)}{(g(-2))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-1)}{(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
